    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Power_Transmission_Network\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    n_nodes = 5\n    nodes = list(range(n_nodes))\n    n_generators = 2\n\n    # Node coordinates (x, y) in km\n    node_coords = [(0, 48), (16, 25), (55, 35), (28, 73), (50, 19)]\n\n    # Node types\n    node_types = ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer']\n\n    # Power demands and generator capacities (MW)\n    power_demands = [0.0, 0.0, 35.0, 44.0, 31.0]\n    generator_capacities = [118.0, 150.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]\n\n    # Wire physical properties (INDUCED PARAMETERS)\n    resistivity = 1.68e-08  # Ohm-meter\n    wire_area_mm2 = 500.0  # mm^2\n    wire_area_m2 = wire_area_mm2 * 1e-6  # Convert to m^2\n\n    # Operating parameters\n    operating_voltage = 220.0  # kV\n    line_capacity = 100.0  # MW\n    build_cost_per_km = 100000.0  # $/km\n    loss_cost_rate = 50.0  # $/MWh equivalent\n\n    # COMPUTE distance and resistance matrices (INDUCED COSTS)\n    # Using Ohm's Law: R = resistivity * length / area\n    def compute_distance_km(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    def compute_resistance(dist_km):\n        dist_m = dist_km * 1000  # Convert km to m\n        return resistivity * dist_m / wire_area_m2\n\n    distances = {}  # km\n    resistances = {}  # Ohm\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                dist = compute_distance_km(node_coords[i], node_coords[j])\n                distances[(i, j)] = dist\n                resistances[(i, j)] = compute_resistance(dist)\n\n    # Create edge list (undirected, i < j)\n    edges = [(i, j) for i in nodes for j in nodes if i < j]\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Build_i_j: Binary - whether to build line between nodes i and j\n    build = {}\n    for (i, j) in edges:\n        build[(i, j)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Build_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Flow_i_j: Continuous - power flow from node i to j (MW)\n    flow = {}\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                flow[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Flow_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Helper function to get build variable (handles i > j case)\n    def get_build(i, j):\n        if i < j:\n            return build[(i, j)]\n        else:\n            return build[(j, i)]\n\n    # Objective: Minimize construction cost + operational transmission cost\n    # Cost factor: converts resistance to cost coefficient\n    # Cost = LossRate * (R / V^2) * P^2\n    # factor = LossRate / V^2\n    loss_factor = loss_cost_rate / (operating_voltage ** 2)\n\n    # Construction costs (based on distance)\n    obj = quicksum(distances[(i, j)] * build_cost_per_km * build[(i, j)] for (i, j) in edges)\n\n    # Operational costs: Quadratic (I^2*R losses)\n    # Note: Gurobi supports quadratic objectives directly\n    obj += quicksum(\n        resistances[(i, j)] * loss_factor * flow[(i, j)] * flow[(i, j)]\n        for i in nodes for j in nodes if i != j\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Flow conservation constraints\n    for node in nodes:\n        inflow = quicksum(flow[(other, node)] for other in nodes if other != node)\n        outflow = quicksum(flow[(node, other)] for other in nodes if other != node)\n        \n        if node_types[node] == 'generator':\n            # Generator: net outflow (generation) <= capacity\n            # Rewritten: inflow - outflow >= -capacity\n            m.addConstr(\n                inflow - outflow >= -generator_capacities[node],\n                name=f\"Gen_Cap_{node}\"\n            )\n        else:\n            # Consumer: net inflow >= demand\n            m.addConstr(\n                inflow - outflow >= power_demands[node],\n                name=f\"Demand_{node}\"\n            )\n\n    # Capacity constraints: Flow <= line_capacity * Build\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    flow[(i, j)] <= line_capacity * get_build(i, j),\n                    name=f\"Cap_{i}_{j}\"\n                )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Planning memo \u2014 five\u2011node transmission build connecting two generators and three consumer loads\n\nWe are planning a new high\u2011voltage transmission layout to connect two generation sites to three consumer load points. There are five nodes in total. We do not have predefined corridors; any pair of nodes can be connected if it is cost\u2011effective to do so. The nodes are positioned at map coordinates given by Node Coordinates (km):\n  - N0 (generator): (0, 48)\n  - N1 (generator): (16, 25)\n  - N2 (consumer): (55, 35)\n  - N3 (consumer): (28, 73)\n  - N4 (consumer): (50, 19), and their functional roles (generation vs. load) are identified in ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer'].\n\nPower needs at the three consumer locations are specified in Consumer demands: N2: 35.0 MW, N3: 44.0 MW, N4: 31.0 MW. Each generator has a maximum production capability listed in Generator capacities: N0: 118.0 MW, N1: 150.0 MW. Our planning target is to route enough power so that every consumer\u2019s demand is at least met; oversupply is acceptable if it proves cost\u2011optimal. Generation dispatch must stay within the stated limits.\n\nIf we elect to construct a line between any two nodes, it will be able to carry power in either direction, constrained by a symmetric rating of 100.0 MW. The cost to build a line is proportional to its route length, using the straight\u2011line distance between the node coordinates as a proxy, at a unit rate of $100,000/km. The design problem is to choose which lines to build and how to route power so that we minimize the combined spending on new construction and ongoing heating costs.\n\nBecause we are using copper conductors, resistive losses are significant and must be modeled realistically. Conductor resistance for any candidate line grows with length and depends on the copper material property in 1.68e-08 \u03a9\u00b7m (copper) and the chosen cross\u2011section in 500.0 mm\u00b2. The operating point for the network is set by 220.0 kV. At that voltage, resistive heating on a line follows the square law: the loss scales with the line\u2019s resistance and the square of the power flow divided by the square of the voltage. We monetize these heating losses at $50.0/MWh. In practical terms, doubling the power flow on a line leads to roughly four times the resistive loss cost, while operating at higher voltage reduces this cost sharply because of the 1/V^2 effect. This is why we avoid linear approximations and why spreading power flows or shortening routes can be more economical than pushing large flows through a single long corridor.\n\nFor planning purposes, we treat power flow at the nodes as perfectly conserved: what enters a node must leave it or be consumed by demand or generation without deducting physical losses from the nodal balance. Resistive losses are accounted for separately as a financial penalty (we pay for the energy that turns into heat), not as a subtraction from delivered power. Within that framework:\n- Power can only move along lines we choose to build.\n- Each built line can carry flow in either direction up to 100.0 MW.\n- Each consumer must receive at least its required demand from Consumer demands: N2: 35.0 MW, N3: 44.0 MW, N4: 31.0 MW.\n- Each generator\u2019s output cannot exceed its limit in Generator capacities: N0: 118.0 MW, N1: 150.0 MW.\n- Construction cost is distance\u2011based at $100,000/km, and operational loss cost follows the quadratic I^2R relationship described above.\n\nThe objective is to minimize the total lifecycle cost we can quantify here: the sum of new line construction costs and the quadratic, voltage\u2011dependent heating costs driven by actual power flows. This approach will naturally penalize concentrated, high\u2011current transfers and reward designs that use shorter paths, adequate conductor size, and the specified voltage to keep resistive losses in check while reliably serving all loads.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Planning memo \u2014 five\u2011node transmission build connecting two generators and three consumer loads\n\nWe are planning a new high\u2011voltage transmission layout to connect two generation sites to three consumer load points. There are five nodes in total. We do not have predefined corridors; any pair of nodes can be connected if it is cost\u2011effective to do so. The nodes are positioned at map coordinates given by {NODE_COORDINATES}, and their functional roles (generation vs. load) are identified in {NODE_TYPES}.\n\nPower needs at the three consumer locations are specified in {POWER_DEMANDS}. Each generator has a maximum production capability listed in {GENERATOR_CAPACITIES}. Our planning target is to route enough power so that every consumer\u2019s demand is at least met; oversupply is acceptable if it proves cost\u2011optimal. Generation dispatch must stay within the stated limits.\n\nIf we elect to construct a line between any two nodes, it will be able to carry power in either direction, constrained by a symmetric rating of {LINE_CAPACITY}. The cost to build a line is proportional to its route length, using the straight\u2011line distance between the node coordinates as a proxy, at a unit rate of {BUILD_COST_PER_KM}. The design problem is to choose which lines to build and how to route power so that we minimize the combined spending on new construction and ongoing heating costs.\n\nBecause we are using copper conductors, resistive losses are significant and must be modeled realistically. Conductor resistance for any candidate line grows with length and depends on the copper material property in {WIRE_RESISTIVITY} and the chosen cross\u2011section in {WIRE_CROSS_SECTION}. The operating point for the network is set by {OPERATING_VOLTAGE}. At that voltage, resistive heating on a line follows the square law: the loss scales with the line\u2019s resistance and the square of the power flow divided by the square of the voltage. We monetize these heating losses at {LOSS_COST_RATE}. In practical terms, doubling the power flow on a line leads to roughly four times the resistive loss cost, while operating at higher voltage reduces this cost sharply because of the 1/V^2 effect. This is why we avoid linear approximations and why spreading power flows or shortening routes can be more economical than pushing large flows through a single long corridor.\n\nFor planning purposes, we treat power flow at the nodes as perfectly conserved: what enters a node must leave it or be consumed by demand or generation without deducting physical losses from the nodal balance. Resistive losses are accounted for separately as a financial penalty (we pay for the energy that turns into heat), not as a subtraction from delivered power. Within that framework:\n- Power can only move along lines we choose to build.\n- Each built line can carry flow in either direction up to {LINE_CAPACITY}.\n- Each consumer must receive at least its required demand from {POWER_DEMANDS}.\n- Each generator\u2019s output cannot exceed its limit in {GENERATOR_CAPACITIES}.\n- Construction cost is distance\u2011based at {BUILD_COST_PER_KM}, and operational loss cost follows the quadratic I^2R relationship described above.\n\nThe objective is to minimize the total lifecycle cost we can quantify here: the sum of new line construction costs and the quadratic, voltage\u2011dependent heating costs driven by actual power flows. This approach will naturally penalize concentrated, high\u2011current transfers and reward designs that use shorter paths, adequate conductor size, and the specified voltage to keep resistive losses in check while reliably serving all loads."
